<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Paul von Hindenburg</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1847">1847</a>–<a href="/years/1934">1934</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Paul von Hindenburg was a German field marshal and president of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar-republic">Weimar Republic</a> from 1925 to 1934. With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erich-ludendorff">Erich Ludendorff</a> he won the Battle of Tannenberg in 1914 and commanded in the east, where the occupied Ober Ost was named after his post, before directing the whole German war effort. As president he appointed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolf-hitler">Adolf Hitler</a> chancellor in January 1933.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/paul-von-hindenburg.jpg" alt="Paul von Hindenburg" width="320" height="429" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author. · CC BY-SA 3.0 de · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-C06886,_Paul_v._Hindenburg_(cropped)(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Hindenburg was born on 2 October 1847 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poznan">Posen</a>, then in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Kingdom of Prussia</a>, into a family of minor Prussian nobility. He enlisted in the Third Regiment of Foot Guards after his cadet education. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-prussian-war">Austro-Prussian War</a> of 1866 he was knocked unconscious at Königgrätz when a bullet pierced his helmet, but wrapped his head in a towel and resumed command of his detachment.</p><p>He served in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franco-prussian-war">Franco-Prussian War</a> as a battalion adjutant and attended the proclamation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/versailles">Versailles</a> on 18 January 1871. He entered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prussian-war-academy">Kriegsakademie</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> in 1873, joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-general-staff">General Staff</a>, rose to lieutenant general in 1900 and retired in 1911.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Living in retirement in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hannover</a> when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> began, he was selected on 22 August 1914 to command the German Eighth Army in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-prussia">East Prussia</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erich-ludendorff">Erich Ludendorff</a> as his chief of staff, after the previous commander Maximilian von Prittwitz had panicked. He became a field marshal on 1 November 1914. In February 1915 an attack encircled an entire Russian corps and captured more than 100,000 men in the Second Battle of the Masurian Lakes.</p><p>In 1916 he replaced Erich von Falkenhayn as Chief of the Great General Staff. He and Ludendorff used the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaiser">Kaiser</a>'s extensive delegation of authority to the Supreme Army Command to establish a de facto military <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a>. In 1917 they pressed Chancellor Bethmann Hollweg to accept unrestricted submarine warfare. After the armistice Hindenburg stepped down and retired again in 1919.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-ebert">Friedrich Ebert</a>'s death on 28 February 1925, the parties of the right chose Hindenburg as their candidate, and he won the second round with the support of the Bavarian People's Party and because the Communists refused to withdraw their candidate. He took office on 12 May 1925.</p><p>In March 1930, on the advice of Kurt von Schleicher, he appointed Heinrich Brüning chancellor of a presidential government, which the historian Eberhard Jäckel judged within the letter of the constitution but against its spirit. At 84 he was persuaded to run again in 1932, supported by parties that saw him as the only hope of defeating Hitler.</p><p>Opposed to Hitler, he nevertheless reluctantly agreed in January 1933 to appoint him chancellor in a coalition cabinet. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichstag">Reichstag</a> fire of February 1933 he approved the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichstag-fire-decree">Reichstag Fire Decree</a>, which suspended civil liberties, and he signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enabling-act-of-1933">Enabling Act of 1933</a>. He died on 2 August 1934, after which Hitler combined the presidency with the chancellery.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Paul von Hindenburg” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On 30 January 1933 President <b>Paul von Hindenburg</b> appointed Hitler chancellor at the head of a coalition cabinet in which conservatives such as Franz von Papen expected to control him.</blockquote><a href="/empire/nazi-germany">Nazi Germany · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>From March 1930 President <b>Paul von Hindenburg</b> used Article 48 to back chancellors without a parliamentary majority: Heinrich Brüning, Franz von Papen and Kurt von Schleicher.</blockquote><a href="/empire/weimar-republic">Weimar Republic · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Weimar Republic chancellors were repeatedly voted out by majorities that could agree on no successor, until President <b>Paul von Hindenburg</b> governed through chancellors ruling by emergency decree, and in 1933 the Reichstag voted away its own powers.</blockquote><a href="/government/parliamentary-democracy">Parliamentary democracy · Weaknesses</a></li><li><blockquote>President <b>Paul von Hindenburg</b> died, and a law passed the day before merged the presidency with the chancellorship.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-2">2 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>After the German offensives of 1915 drove Russian armies out of Courland, Lithuania and the Grodno-Białystok region, the territory was placed under the Supreme Commander in the East, Oberbefehlshaber Ost, <b>Paul von Hindenburg</b>, with Erich Ludendorff as chief of staff.</blockquote><a href="/state/ober-ost">Ober Ost</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in 6 Podgórna Street in Poznań, in what is now <a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a>. Died in Ogrodzieniec, in what is now <a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=52.41,16.93&amp;year=1903" aria-label="6 Podgórna Street in Poznań on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4930 355 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Paul von Hindenburg"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5380 625h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5380 625h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=52.41,16.93&amp;year=1903">What was here in 1903? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
